==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|lendare}}, {{m|enm|leendare}}, variants of {{m|enm|lenner}}, {{m|enm|lenere}}, equivalent to {{affix|en|lend|-er|id2=agent noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɛndɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lender.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛndə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who [[lend]]s, especially [[money]]; specifically, a [[bank]] or other [[entity]] that specializes in granting [[loan]]s.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|1|3||passage=[Polonius]: Neither a borrower nor a '''lender''' be:<br>For loan oft loses both itself and friend;<br>And borrowing dulls the edge of husbandry.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=71|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show End of the peer show]
|passage=Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of peer-to-peer lending comes close. This is an industry that brings together individual savers and '''lenders''' on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to lend.}}

